  "tldr": "For a known acyclic ADMG with compact mixed variables, an abstract district-coupled response sieve yields a two-sided exact-coded-law lifting and exact finite-sample coverage of the full Lipschitz-class identified set, with excess width bounded by the coded-law endpoint modulus plus an explicit linear mesh term. Computation is a separate theorem: rational support boxes, semialgebraic code cells, and a specified rational piecewise-polynomial or ReLU mechanism-and-query grammar produce a finite semialgebraic program with an independently checkable branch-and-bound certificate. Certified near-extremal rational neural witnesses form an inner interval whose residual relative to the computable class is explicitly defined and separated from sieve, optimization, and witness tolerances.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Tan, Blanchet, and Syrgkanis establish mixed-variable neural approximation and asymptotic consistency but leave finite-sample latent-confounded guarantees open. Definition 2.3 and Theorem 2.4 of Zhang, Tian, and Bareinboim and the Autobounds program cover finite response domains, but neither supplies a quantitative exact-coded-law lifting for compact mixed variables. Existing global-optimization work does not state the rational causal input grammar needed for independent certificates.",
    "niche": "The mathematical validity problem and the computational certification problem require different scopes. The full Lipschitz class supports a noncomputational approximation and coverage theorem; a rationally encoded subclass supports a finite semialgebraic program, target extrema, and reproducible certificates.",
    "fill": "The project proves the full-class two-sided lifting and exact whole-set coverage, then proves finite termination and soundness only for rational boxes, semialgebraic code cells, and rational piecewise-polynomial or ReLU mechanisms and queries. Near-extremal neural witnesses are checked against explicit neural extrema, and their formal residual is separated from mesh and optimization errors."
  },
  "related_work": "Tan, Blanchet, and Syrgkanis motivate mixed-variable neural SCM approximation, but their latent-confounded results do not provide exact finite-sample whole-set coverage. Zhang, Tian, and Bareinboim's Definition 2.3 introduces canonical SCMs, and their Theorem 2.4 establishes sufficiency for finite-domain counterfactual distributions; the full-class lifting here is a quantitative exact-coded-law analogue for compact mixed domains, without claiming that arbitrary Lipschitz inputs form a finite semialgebraic program. Choe et al. quotient finite canonical domains and inform the discrete side of the construction. Duarte et al. provide anytime numerical outer bounds and solver-reported epsilon sharpness for discrete causal polynomial programs; the computable theorem tightens the trust boundary only on the declared rational grammar by producing independently checkable rational certificates. Chafai and Concordet provide the exact multinomial inversion, while Romano and Shaikh supply the whole-identified-set coverage criterion under asymptotic calibration. Xia et al., Balazadeh et al., and Tan et al. motivate graph-compatible neural witnesses, but do not certify exact coded-law feasibility together with explicit near-extremality. Cheung, Gleixner, and Steffy, Cook et al., Bunel et al., and Shi et al. inform the certificate calculus. Rahman et al. provide the Parents' Labor Supply decision consumer and an approximate neural decomposition of sampling and nonidentification uncertainty.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "Exact finite-sample coverage concerns the identified set restricted by the fixed finite code \\(\\kappa\\), not the identified set under the entire unbinned continuous observational law. The interval \\(I_{\\mathrm{abs}}\\) is valid over the full Lipschitz class but need not be computable. The executable interval \\(I_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) covers only the explicitly declared rationally certifiable subclass. No inclusion or limiting relation is asserted between the separate atomic bow diagnostic and the bounded-density continuous-noise class. Fixed-tolerance certificate termination does not imply polynomial runtime.",
  "honest_scope": "The exact multinomial inversion is settled once deterministic outer lifting is available. The central theorem target is the two-sided exact-coded-law response lifting over the full Lipschitz class and its linear endpoint error. Rational branch-and-bound soundness and finite termination are claimed only for rational compact boxes, semialgebraic coding cells, and the specified rational piecewise-polynomial or ReLU grammar. The binary bow result is a separate discrete atomic-response sanity proposition. Vanishing neural representation residual \\(\\Gamma_n\\) remains open; every reported neural witness must nevertheless carry an independently checked feasibility and near-extremality tolerance.",
